The pulvinar nuclei are association nuclei involved in visuospatial processing, attention, and higher-order cognitive functions. 

Their subdivisions have distinct roles:

– the lateral and inferior pulvinar nuclei are primarily involved in visual processing via connections with occipitotemporal cortex; 

– the medial pulvinar nuclei connect with frontoparietotemporal association cortex;

– the anterior pulvinar nuclei connect with parietal somatosensory areas and the insula.

The mediodorsal nucleus is a large nucleus on the medial surface of the thalamus, medial to the internal medullary lamina. It comprises three subdivisions: 

– a smaller anteromedial magnocellular division connected with the amygdala and orbitofrontal cortex; 

– a larger posterolateral parvocellular division connected reciprocally with the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex, anterior cingulate gyrus, and supplementary motor area; 

– a paralaminar division (also termed the multiform or densocellular part), lying adjacent to the internal medullary lamina.

The internal medullary lamina separates the major thalamic nuclear groups: 

– the anterior nuclear group lies within its anterior bifurcation; 
– the mediodorsal and midline/periventricular nuclei lie medial to it; 
– the lateral and dorsal nuclear groups lie lateral to it. 

The intralaminar nuclei are embedded within the lamina itself.

The columns of the fornix deliver hippocampal efferent output to subcortical targets: 

– postcommissural fibers project to the mammillary bodies, contributing to the medial limbic circuit (of Papez) and supporting episodic and recollective memory; 

– precommissural fibers project to the septal nuclei and hypothalamus, mediating emotional and autonomic responses.

The tectum functions through its two constituent structures: 

– the superior colliculi integrate visual and other sensory input to coordinate reflexive eye and head movements;
– the inferior colliculi are relay stations in the auditory pathway and contribute to the startle response.

Four recesses project from the main cavity, namely the: 
– supraoptic recess above the optic chiasm;
– infundibular recess extending into the pituitary stalk;
– suprapineal recess above the pineal gland;
– pineal recess extending into the stalk of the pineal gland.

Each white communicating branch carries preganglionic sympathetic fibers that will do one of four things:

—synapse at a sympathetic chain ganglion at the same level; 

—ascend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a higher vertebral level;

—descend the sympathetic trunk and synapse in a chain ganglion at a lower vertebral level;

—exit the sympathetic trunk without synapsing (splanchnic nerve). 

Postganglionic neurons that synapse with the preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the white rami will target blood vessels, sweat glands, and arrector pili muscles throughout the body wall, head, neck, and limbs, or in the case of splanchnic nerves, visceral organs.

