The auricular skin tightly wraps around the cartilaginous ridges (elevations) and sulci (depressions) of the external ear including the helix, antihelix, crura of the helix, the fossa triangularis, tragus, and antitragus, to name a few.

The auricular skin continues into the concha; which is the hollow next to the ear canal and continues with the skin of the external auditory canal, until it reaches the tympanic membrane.

Inferiorly, the skin makes up the earlobe, whose core comprises tough areolar and adipose connective tissue. The cartilage is lacking in this region.

Cutaneous sensory innervation to the auricular skin comes from various sources, including the:

- greater auricular nerve, which innervates the superficial skin in the anteroinferior and posteroinferior portions of the external ear;

- lesser occipital nerve, which originates from the cervical plexus and innervates the superficial skin in the posterosuperior portion of the external ear;

- auriculotemporal nerve, which innervates the superficial skin in the anterosuperior portion of the external ear;

- auricular branches of the vagus and facial nerves, which innervates the auricular skin in the deeper regions including the concha.

Arterial supply to auricular skin comes from various sources, including the:

- posterior auricular artery (branch of the external carotid artery);

- anterior auricular branches of the superficial temporal artery;

- occipital artery.

The veins follow the corresponding arterial branches.

Lymphatic drainage from the auricular skin drains anteriorly into parotid nodes, while posteriorly into mastoid lymph nodes. Additional drainage into the upper deep cervical lymph nodes have been also reported (Standring, 2016).

The female external urethral sphincter muscle is one of the muscles of the deep perineal space, which itself is found in the urogenital triangle. It is a large, circular muscle that partly surrounds both the urethra and vagina in females.

It is located:

- anterior to the perineal body;

- superior to the perineal membrane;

- inferior to the urinary bladder.

For descriptive purposes, the female external urethral sphincter muscle is divided into three parts:

- a superiorly located sphincter urethrae muscle;

- an anteriorly located compressor urethrae muscle;

- a posteriorly located sphincter urethrovaginalis muscle (Jung, Ahn and Huh, 2012).

The female external urethral sphincter muscle is one of the muscles of the deep perineal space, which itself is found in the urogenital triangle. It is a large, circular muscle that partly surrounds both the urethra and vagina in females.

It is located:

