The labia majora have an outer pigmented surface that develops hair and an inner smooth surface that contain sebaceous follicles. Between these layers there is loose connective tissue intermixed with adipocytes and smooth muscle cells, as well as nerves and vessels. There are two layers of subcutaneous fascia:

- a superficial layer analogous to the superficial fatty layers (or Camper’s fascia) of the anterior abdominal wall;

- a deeper membranous layer (or Colles’ fascia) similar to the membranous layer (or Scarpa’s fascia) of the anterior abdominal wall.

The subcortical white matter (semioval center) is the large mass of myelinated nerve fibers occupying each cerebral hemisphere deep to the cerebral cortex. It takes its name from its semioval appearance in horizontal section, as described by Vieussens (1684). The basal nuclei are partly embedded within it, and it is continuous inferiorly with the internal capsule.

The subcortical white matter contains three categories of fibers: 

– projection fibers, which connect the cortex to subcortical structures and form the corona radiata as they converge toward the internal capsule; 

– association fibers, which connect cortical areas within the same hemisphere, including short U-fibers running superficially between adjacent gyri and longer fasciculi running deeper; 

– commissural fibers, principally those of the corpus callosum, which connect corresponding areas of the two hemispheres. 

Major tracts, including the corticospinal tract, superior longitudinal fasciculus, and corpus callosum fibers , intersect within the semioval center.

The tunica vaginalis is the outermost of three layers that surround the testis. It is a continuation of the processus vaginalis, which is formed prior to the descent of the fetal testes. When the testes descend from the abdomen to the pelvis, the tunica vaginalis forms two layers: 

—the visceral layer that surrounds the tunica albuginea (except for the posterior portion of the testis);

—the parietal layer that surrounds and extends proximally over a portion of the spermatic cord.

