The fibers of the periodontal ligament are predominantly made from collagen and can be largely classified into 3 groups: principal fibers, accessory fibers, and oxytalan fibers. The principal fibers of the periodontal ligament are described as wavy collagen bundles. The principal fibers can be further divided into three ligaments: 

—gingival fibers; 

—transseptal or interdental fibers; 

—alveolodental ligament (Rajendran and Selvam, 2015).

The transseptal fibers form a dense ligament between adjacent teeth, forming a continuous chain-like structure between them. The fibers form horizontal bundles that are specifically located apical to the junctional epithelium, over the alveolar crest. The fibers extend between the cement of adjacent teeth into which they are embedded (Rajendran and Selvam, 2015).

The diploic veins are a valveless intraosseous network of veins draining the skull itself. These are situated in the diploe, the spongy layer of bone in the cranial vault, and run inside the diploic channels.

The diploic veins communicate with the dural venous sinuses and meningeal veins along the compact inner table of the skull and with the pericranial veins on the outer table via emissary veins (Jivraj et al., 2009; Garcia-Gonzalez et al., 2009).

The diploic veins can be categorized into four groups based on their location in the human skull:

—frontal; 

—anterior temporal;

—posterior temporal;

—occipital.

The emissary veins are small veins which pass through various foramina inside the skull to connect the intracranial venous sinuses with the extracranial veins, often via a network of diploic veins. Learning their anatomy has clinical implications as these venous channels could provide a route for the spread of infection from outside the cranium into the venous sinuses intracranially. For instance, the spread of infection from the paranasal sinuses to the cavernous sinus.

Examples of a few important emissary veins have been given below:

—mastoid emissary vein interconnects auricular or occipital veins from suboccipital venous plexus with the sigmoid sinus;

—parietal emissary vein interconnects the veins of the scalp with the superior sagittal sinus;

—condylar emissary vein interconnects the sigmoid sinus and veins of the suboccipital triangle;

—occipital emissary vein interconnects the conﬂuence of sinuses with the occipital vein;

—venous plexus of foramen ovale interconnects the cavernous sinus and the pterygoid plexus of veins;

—internal carotid venous plexus interconnects the internal jugular vein and cavernous sinus;

—an emissary vein through the sphenoidal foramen (of Vesalius) and the venous plexus of foramen lacerum interconnect the cavernous sinus and the pharyngeal veins and pterygoid plexus.

The ophthalmic veins are potentially emissary veins, as they interconnect intra- and extracranial veins.

It should be noted that emissary veins are valveless channels and, hence, the venous blood can flow in either direction (Tubbs, Shoja and Loukas, 2016; Mortazavi et al., 2012).

The hypophyseal fossa (hypophysial fossa, pituitary fossa, or sellar fossa) is the deep depression found along the upper aspect of the body of sphenoid bone. The hypophyseal fossa;

—forms the middle portion of the sella turcica;

—is located anterior to the dorsum sellae, posterior to the tuberculum sellae, and inferior to the diaphragma sellae;

—lodges the pituitary gland.

The dorsum sellae is the quadrilateral, superiorly projecting bony plate found along the upper aspect of the body of sphenoid bone. The dorsum sellae:

—forms the posterior portion of the sella turcica;

—is located anterior to the clivus and posterior to the hypophyseal fossa;

—consists of posterior clinoid processes on its superolateral angles;

—provides attachment sites for the tentorium cerebelli and diaphragma sellae.

The medial plates (medial laminae or medial pterygoid plates) are the thin, curved, inferiorly projecting bony plates found along the medial aspects of both the right and left pterygoid processes of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each medial plate:

—consists of a scaphoid fossa, vaginal process and pterygoid hamulus;

—contributes to the formation of a pterygoid fossa and the lateral wall and choana of a nasal cavity;

—provides origin sites for a tensor veli palatini and superior pharyngeal constrictor muscles, as well as an attachment site for a pterygomandibular raphe;

—articulates with the vomer and a palatine bone.

The lateral plates (lateral laminae or lateral pterygoid plates) are the thin, curved, inferiorly projecting bony plates found along the lateral aspects of both the right and left pterygoid processes of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each lateral plate:

—consists of a pterygospinous process of sphenoid bone;

—contributes to the formation of pterygoid and infratemporal fossae;

—provides origin sites for medial and lateral pterygoid muscles, as well as an attachment site for a pterygospinous ligament;

—articulates with a palatine bone.

The body of sphenoid bone is the hollow, cube-like, centrally located part of the bone. It is continuous;

—superolaterally with both the right and left lesser wings of sphenoid bone;

—laterally with both the right and left greater wings of sphenoid bone;

—inferolaterally with both the right and left pterygoid processes of sphenoid bone.

The body of sphenoid bone consists of:

—anterior, posterior and inferior surfaces;

—a single sphenoidal yoke, sella turcica, limbus, chiasmatic sulcus and ethmoidal spine;

—a pair of carotid sulci, sphenoidal lingulae, petrosal processes and sphenoidal sinuses;

—the anterior portion of the clivus.

The body of sphenoid bone:

—articulates with the vomer, ethmoid, occipital, temporal and palatine bones;

—contributes to the formation of the anterior and middle cranial fossae, the medial walls of both the right and left orbits, and both the right and left nasal cavities;

—contributes to the formation of the right and left optic canals, superior orbital fissures and foramina lacera.

The lesser wings (minor wings, alae parvae, Ingrassia’s wings, or Ingrassia’s processes) are the two thin, horizontal, triangular processes found along the superolateral aspects of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each lesser wing:

—is continuous inferomedially with the body of sphenoid bone;

—consists of superior and inferior surfaces, and anterior and posterior margins;

—articulates with the frontal bone;

—contributes to the formation of the anterior cranial fossa and the roof of an orbit;

—contributes to the formation of an optic canal and superior orbital fissure.

The greater wings (major wings or alae magnae) are the two large, curved, irregular processes found along the lateral aspects of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each greater wing:

—is continuous medially with the body of sphenoid bone and continuous inferomedially with a pterygoid process of sphenoid bone;

—consists of cerebral, temporal, infratemporal, maxillary and orbital surfaces, frontal, parietal, squamosal, petrosal and zygomatic margins, and an infratemporal crest, spine of sphenoid bone and sulcus of auditory tube;

—consists of a foramen rotundum, foramen ovale and foramen spinosum, and contributes to the formation of superior and inferior orbital fissures;

—articulates with frontal, parietal, temporal and zygomatic bones;

—contributes to the formation of the middle cranial fossa, temporal fossa, infratemporal fossa, and lateral wall of an orbit;

—provides origin sites for a temporalis muscle and a superior head of lateral pterygoid muscle;

—provides attachments sites for pterygospinous and sphenomandibular ligaments.

The pterygoid processes are the two vertical, curved, irregular processes found along the inferolateral aspects of the sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each pterygoid process:

—is continuous superomedially with the body of sphenoid bone and continuous superolaterally with a greater wing of sphenoid bone;

—consists of a medial plate, lateral plate and pterygoid fossa;

—contributes to the formation of a pterygoid canal, infratemporal fossa, and the lateral wall and choana of a nasal cavity;

—provides origin sites for tensor veli palatini, medial pterygoid, lateral pterygoid and superior pharyngeal constrictor muscles, as well as attachment sites for a pterygospinous ligament and pterygomandibular raphe;

—articulates with the vomer and a palatine bone.

The carotid sulci (carotid grooves) are the two shallow, curved depressions found along both the right and left lateral aspects of the body of sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each carotid sulcus:

—travels posteriorly from the medial end of a lesser wing of sphenoid bone towards a foramen lacerum and carotid canal;

—consists of a sphenoidal lingula at its posterolateral end;

—lodges an internal carotid artery.

The spines of sphenoid bone (sphenoidal spines, or angular spines of sphenoid bone) are the two pointed, posteroinferiorly projecting processes found along the posterior aspects of each of the greater wings of sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each spine of sphenoid bone;

—is found at the point where the squamosal and petrosal margins of a greater wing of sphenoid bone meet;

—is located posterior to a foramen spinosum and lateral to a sulcus of auditory tube;

—extends into the angle formed between the anterior aspects of the squamous and petrous parts of a temporal bone;

—contributes to the formation of both a sphenosquamous suture and sphenopetrosal synchondrosis;

—provides attachments sites for sphenomandibular and pterygospinous ligaments.

The pterygoid hamuli (pterygoid hooks) are the two hook-like, inferolateral projections found along the inferior ends of both the right and left medial plates of the pterygoid processes of sphenoid bone. On its corresponding side, each pterygoid hamulus:

—consists of a groove, through which the tendon of a tensor veli palatini muscle passes;

—provides an origin site for a superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle and an attachment site for a pterygomandibular raphe.

The lesser horns (lesser cornua) are the two small, conical projections found along the superior aspect of the hyoid bone. On its corresponding side, each lesser horn of hyoid bone:

—extends posterosuperiorly from the junction between the body and greater horn of the hyoid bone; 

—provides origin sites for middle pharyngeal constrictor and chondroglossus muscles;

—provides an attachment site for the stylohyoid ligament.

The greater horns (greater cornua) are the two thin, oblique, posterolaterally located parts of the hyoid bone. On its corresponding side, each greater horn of hyoid bone:

—extends posterosuperiorly from a lateral end of the body of hyoid bone; 

—is continuous superomedially with a lesser horn of hyoid bone;

—consists of a tubercle of hyoid bone;

—provides an origin site for middle pharyngeal constrictor and hyoglossus muscles and an insertion site for a thyrohyoid muscle;

—provides attachment sites for the thyrohyoid membrane and a lateral thyrohyoid ligament.

The transverse processes are the two small, laterally projecting processes of the atlas (first cervical vertebra). On its corresponding side, each transverse process of atlas:

—consists of anterior and posterior roots that extend from a lateral mass;

—consists of a foramen transversarium;

—provides attachment sites for muscles of the neck and upper back.

The perpendicular plate (perpendicular part, vertical plate, or vertical part) is the vertical, flat, bony plate of the palatine bone. It is one of the two bony plates of the palatine bone, the other being the horizontal plate, and it is continuous:

—superiorly with the orbital and sphenoidal processes;

—inferomedially with the horizontal plate;

—inferolaterally with the pyramidal process.

The perpendicular plate consists of nasal and maxillary surfaces, and anterior and posterior borders. It articulates with the maxilla, inferior nasal concha, ethmoid, and sphenoid bones.

The palatine process is the thick, horizontal, medial projection found along the inferomedial aspect of the maxilla. The palatine process is:

—continuous laterally with the body of maxilla;

—continuous both anteriorly and laterally with the alveolar process of maxilla;

—consists of the nasal crest and palatine grooves.

The palatine process articulates:

—medially, with the palatine process of the opposite maxilla, forming the anterior portion of the median palatine suture;

—posteriorly, with the horizontal plate of the palatine bone, forming the transverse palatine suture;

—superomedially, with the vomer.

The palatine processes of the maxilla combine to form the anterior three quarters of the bony hard palate, while the horizontal plates of the palatine bones combine to form its posterior one quarter. The bony hard palate contributes to the formation of both the floor of the nasal cavity and the roof of the oral cavity.

The pyramidal process is the thick, pointed, posterolateral projection of the palatine bone. It extends from the posterior aspect of the junction between the perpendicular and horizontal plates of the palatine bone. The pyramidal process:

—consists of the lesser palatine foramina;

—provides an origin site for the medial pterygoid muscle;

—articulates with the maxilla and sphenoid bone.

The occlusal (masticatory) surface is the aspect of the mandibular first premolar tooth that faces the teeth of the maxillary dental arcade and comes into contact with them (occlusion) during mastication. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, mesial, and distal surfaces. The occlusal surface consists of marginal ridges and a central groove, which separates the buccal and lingual cusps.

The occlusal surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the buccoocclusal angle;

- lingual surface at the linguoocclusal angle;

- mesial surface at the mesioocclusal angle;

- distal surface at the distoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the occlusal surface of the mandibular premolar teeth can be divided into three portions by two horizontal lines that travel mesiodistally. In a buccal to lingual direction, these are the:

- buccal third;

- middle third;

- lingual third.

Furthermore, the occlusal surface of the mandibular premolar teeth can also be divided into three portions by two horizontal lines that travel buccolingually. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The lingual surface is the aspect of the mandibular first premolar tooth that faces towards the tongue and oral cavity. It is one of five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, mesial, distal, and occlusal surfaces.

The lingual surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- distal surface at the distolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the linguoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the lingual surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- occlusal third;

- middle third;

- cervical third.

Furthermore, the lingual surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The root (anatomical root) is the inferior portion of the mandibular first premolar tooth that is covered by cementum. It is separated from the crown by the cervical line, consists of one apex and is lodged in a dental alveolus of the mandible.

For descriptive purposes, the root of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. From superior to inferior, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- apical third.

The clinical root is a term used to describe the inferior portion of a mandibular tooth that is covered by gingiva when viewed in the oral cavity.

The distal surface is the aspect of the maxillary first premolar tooth that faces towards the maxillary second premolar tooth. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, mesial, and occlusal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The distal surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the distobuccal angle;

- lingual surface at the distolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the distoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the distal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the distal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to buccal direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- buccal third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The buccal surface is the aspect of the maxillary first premolar tooth that faces towards the cheek and vestibule. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the lingual, mesial, distal, and occlusal surfaces. The buccal surface consists of three buccal lobes.

The buccal surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiobuccal angle;

- distal surface at the distobuccal angle;

- occlusal surface at the buccoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the buccal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the buccal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces.

The mylohyoid lines (internal oblique ridges) are the two oblique ridges found along the internal surface of the body of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each mylohyoid line:

—extends anteroinferiorly from the area that lies posteroinferior to the third molar tooth towards the lower portion of the mandibular symphysis, although its posterior end is more distinct than its anterior end;

—is located superior to the submandibular fossa, inferior to the sublingual fossa and anterior to the mylohyoid groove;

—provides an origin site for the mylohyoid muscle.

The lacrimal groove (lacrimal sulcus) is the vertical, deep depression found along the lateral aspect of the lacrimal bone. It is located:

—anterior to the posterior lacrimal crest;

—posteromedial to the lacrimal groove of maxilla;

—superior to the lacrimal process of inferior nasal concha.

The lacrimal groove of lacrimal bone is converted into the nasolacrimal canal by its combination with both the lacrimal groove of the maxilla and the lacrimal process of the inferior nasal concha. This canal allows for the passage of the nasolacrimal duct.

The middle nasal conchae (middle nasal turbinates) are the two curved, anteroinferiorly located, bony plates found along the medial aspects of each of the ethmoidal labyrinths. On its corresponding side, each middle nasal concha:

—is located inferior to a superior nasal concha;

—forms the floor of a superior nasal meatus and the roof of a middle nasal meatus;

—contributes to the formation of the lateral wall of a nasal cavity.

The posterior ethmoidal grooves (posterior ethmoidal sulci) are the two small, horizontal, posteriorly located depressions found along the superolateral aspects of each ethmoidal labyrinth. On its corresponding side, each posterior ethmoidal groove of ethmoid bone:

—travels laterally from the anterior cranial fossa to an orbital cavity;

—is converted into a posterior ethmoidal foramen (posterior ethmoidal canal) by its combination with a posterior ethmoidal groove of frontal bone and this foramen allows for the passage of a posterior ethmoidal nerve and vessels.

The crista galli is the thick, triangular, superiorly projecting process found along the midline on the superior aspect of the ethmoid bone. It is continuous inferomedially with the perpendicular plate and continuous inferolaterally with the cribriform plate. The crista galli:

—consists of right and left alae;

—provides an attachment site for the falx cerebri;

—articulates with the inferomedial aspect of the frontal bone, contributing to the formation of the frontoethmoidal suture;

—contributes to the formation of the foramen cecum and anterior cranial fossa.

The cribriform plate (horizontal plate) is the horizontal, sieve-like plate of bone found along the superior aspect of the ethmoid bone. It is continuous:

—superomedially with the crista galli,

—inferomedially with the perpendicular plate;

—inferolaterally with both the right and left ethmoidal labyrinths.

The cribriform plate:

—consists of many cribriform foramina, which allow for the passage of the olfactory nerves;

—consists of two anteriorly located grooves that lodge the olfactory bulbs;

—contributes to the formation of the anterior cranial fossa and the roofs of the nasal cavities.

The perpendicular plate (perpendicular part) is the vertical, flat, inferiorly projecting bony plate found along the midline on the ethmoid bone. It is continuous superomedially with the crista galli and continuous superolaterally with the cribriform plate. The perpendicular plate: 

—consists of anterior, posterior and inferior borders and right and left lateral surfaces;

—contributes to the formation of the nasal septum;

—provides an attachment site for the septal nasal cartilage;

—articulates with the vomer, frontal, and sphenoid bones.

The superior nasal conchae (superior nasal turbinates) are the two curved, posterosuperiorly located, bony plates found along the medial aspects of each of the ethmoidal labyrinths. On its corresponding side, each superior nasal concha:

—is located superior to a middle nasal concha;

—forms the roof of a superior nasal meatus;

—contributes to the formation of the lateral wall of a nasal cavity.

The anterior ethmoidal grooves (anterior ethmoidal sulci) are the two small, horizontal, anteriorly located depressions found along the superolateral aspects of each ethmoidal labyrinth. On its corresponding side, each anterior ethmoidal groove of ethmoid bone:

—travels laterally from the anterior cranial fossa to an orbital cavity;

—is converted into an anterior ethmoidal foramen (anterior ethmoidal canal) by its combination with an anterior ethmoidal groove of frontal bone and this foramen allows for the passage of an anterior ethmoidal nerve and vessels.

The orbital plate (lamina papyracea) is the thin, smooth area found along the superolateral aspect of the ethmoidal labyrinth. It contributes to the formation of the medial wall of the orbit and articulates:

—anteriorly, with the posterior border of lacrimal bone, forming the ethmoidolacrimal suture;

—posteriorly, with the anterior surface of the body of sphenoid bone, contributing to the formation of the sphenoethmoidal suture;

—superiorly, with the inferomedial aspect of the frontal bone, contributing to the formation of the frontoethmoidal suture;

—anteroinferiorly, with the orbital surface of the maxilla, forming the ethmoidomaxillary suture;

—posteroinferiorly, with the orbital process of palatine bone, forming the palatoethmoidal suture.

The mandibular condyles (heads of mandible) are the two expanded, articular, superior ends of the condylar processes of the mandible. On its corresponding side, the mandibular condyle:

—is continuous inferiorly with a neck of mandible;

—consists of a condylion mediale and condylion laterale;

—contributes to the formation of a temporomandibular joint, along with a mandibular fossa of the temporal bone and an intervening articular disc of temporomandibular joint.

The angles of mandible (mandibular angles or gonial angles) are the two corners found along the posteroinferior aspects of both the right and left rami of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each angle of mandible:

—is located at the point where the posterior and inferior borders of the ramus of mandible meet;

—consists of the gonion;

—provides an insertion site for the masseter and medial pterygoid muscles;

—provides an attachment site for the stylomandibular ligament.

The mandibular notches (sigmoid notches) are the two deep indentations found along the posterosuperior aspects of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each mandibular notch:

—travels anteriorly from a condylar process to a coronoid process of the mandible;

—allows for the passage of the masseteric nerve and vessels.

The coronoid processes are the two triangular, anterosuperiorly located projections found along the superior aspect of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each coronoid process:

—is located anterior to a condylar process;

—is continuous inferiorly with a ramus of mandible;

—consists of a temporal crest of mandible;

—provides an insertion site for a temporalis muscle.

The oblique lines (external oblique ridges) are the two ridges found along the external surface of the body of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each oblique line:

—is continuous superiorly with the anterior border of a ramus of the mandible and extends anteroinferiorly towards a mental tubercle;

—forms the lateral boundary of a retromolar fossa;

—provides an origin site for the depressor anguli oris muscle.

The alveolar part (alveolar process) is the thick, U-shaped, superior projection found along the superior aspect of the mandible. The alveolar part:

—is continuous inferiorly with the body of mandible;

—consists of the dental alveoli, interalveolar septa and interradicular septa of the mandible;

—provides an origin site for the buccinator muscle.

The alveolar part of the mandible forms the mandibular alveolar arch, which lodges all of the mandibular (lower) teeth.

The condylar processes (condyloid processes) are the two articular, posterosuperiorly located projections found along the superior aspect of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each condylar process:

—is located posterior to a coronoid process;

—is continuous inferiorly with a ramus of mandible;

—consists of a head and neck of mandible;

—provides an insertion site for a lateral pterygoid muscle and an attachment site for the lateral ligament of a temporomandibular joint;

—contributes to the formation of a temporomandibular joint, along with a mandibular fossa of temporal bone and an intervening articular disc of temporomandibular joint.

The lingulae of mandible are the two small projections found along the medial surfaces of each ramus of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each lingula of mandibles:

—extends from the anterior margin of a mandibular foramen;

—provides an attachment site for a sphenomandibular ligament.

The body is the centrally located, large, pyramidal part of the maxilla. It consists of the maxillary sinus and four processes extend from it:

—a superiorly located frontal process;

—an inferiorly located alveolar process;

—a laterally located zygomatic process;

—a medially located palatine process.

The frontal process (nasal process) is the thin, long, superior projection found along the anterosuperior aspect of the maxilla. The frontal process:

—is continuous inferiorly with the body of maxilla;

—consists of the anterior lacrimal crest and ethmoidal crest of maxilla;

—contributes to the formation of the lateral wall of the nasal cavity.

The frontal process of maxilla articulates:

—anteriorly, with the lateral border of the nasal bone, forming the nasomaxillary suture;

—superiorly, with the nasal margin of the frontal bone, forming the frontomaxillary suture;

—posteriorly, with the lacrimal bone, contributing to the formation of the lacrimomaxillary suture;

—medially, with the middle nasal concha of ethmoid bone.

The alveolar process (alveolar part) is the thick, curved, inferior projection found along the inferior aspect of the maxilla. The alveolar process of maxilla:

—is continuous superiorly with the body of maxilla;

—is continuous both posteriorly and medially with the palatine process of maxilla;

—consists of the dental alveoli, interalveolar septa and interradicular septa of the maxilla;

—provides an origin site for the buccinator muscle;

—articulates with the alveolar process of the opposite maxilla, contributing to the formation of the intermaxillary suture.

The alveolar processes of both the right and left maxillae combine to form the maxillary alveolar arch, which lodges all of the maxillary (upper) teeth.

The superciliary arches are the two smooth, curved elevations found along the external surface of the squamous part of frontal bone. On its corresponding side, each superciliary arch:

—extends laterally from the glabella and travels towards a zygomatic process of the frontal bone;

—is located superior to a supraorbital margin and inferior to a frontal eminence.

The groove for superior sagittal sinus (sulcus for superior sagittal sinus, or sagittal sulcus) is a shallow depression found along the midline on the internal surfaces of the frontal, parietal and occipital bones.

For descriptive purposes, the groove for superior sagittal sinus can be arbitrarily divided into three portions:

- anterior portion, which is located on the frontal bone and is the superior continuation of its frontal crest that extends posterosuperiorly to its parietal margin;

- middle portion, which is located on both the right and left parietal bones and travels posteriorly along their sagittal borders and then curves in a posteroinferior direction towards the occipital bone;

- posterior portion, which is located on the occipital bone and extends inferiorly from its superior angle towards the internal occipital protuberance, where it becomes continuous with the groove for transverse sinus on the right side of the occipital bone.

The groove for superior sagittal sinus lodges the superior sagittal sinus and its margins provide attachment sites for the falx cerebri.

The groove for superior sagittal sinus (sulcus for superior sagittal sinus, or sagittal sulcus) is a shallow depression found along the midline on the internal surfaces of the frontal, parietal and occipital bones.

For descriptive purposes, the groove for superior sagittal sinus can be arbitrarily divided into three portions:

- anterior portion, which is located on the frontal bone and is the superior continuation of its frontal crest that extends posterosuperiorly to its parietal margin;

- middle portion, which is located on both the right and left parietal bones and travels posteriorly along their sagittal borders and then curves in a posteroinferior direction towards the occipital bone;

- posterior portion, which is located on the occipital bone and extends inferiorly from its superior angle towards the internal occipital protuberance, where it becomes continuous with the groove for transverse sinus on the right side of the occipital bone.

The groove for superior sagittal sinus lodges the superior sagittal sinus and its margins provide attachment sites for the falx cerebri.

The orbital parts (orbital plates) are the two inferolaterally located, horizontal, curved portions of the frontal bone. On its corresponding side, each orbital part:

—is continuous medially with the nasal part and continuous superiorly with the squamous part;

—consists of orbital and cerebral surfaces and a sphenoidal margin;

—contributes to the formation of the roof of an orbit and the anterior cranial fossa;

—articulates posteriorly with the greater and lesser wings of sphenoid bone, forming a sphenofrontal suture;

—articulates inferomedially, with the superior aspect of the ethmoid bone, contributing to the formation of the frontoethmoidal suture.

The internal occipital protuberance is the prominence found along the internal aspect of the squamous part of occipital bone. It is located at the center of the cruciform eminence, where the:

—groove for the superior sagittal sinus extends superiorly from it;

—right and left grooves for transverse sinuses extend laterally from it;

—internal occipital crest extends inferiorly from it.

The squamous part is the anterolaterally located, thin, curved portion of the temporal bone. It is continuous:

—medially, with the petrous part;

—inferiorly, with the tympanic part;

—posteriorly, with the mastoid part. 

The squamous part consists of:

—two surfaces (cerebral and temporal);

—two borders (sphenoidal and parietal);

—a zygomatic process.

It forms the anterior, articular portion of the mandibular fossa and the superior and posterosuperior walls of the external acoustic meatus.

The squamous part articulates:

—anteriorly, with the squamosal margin of the greater wing of sphenoid bone, forming the sphenosquamous suture;

—posteriorly, with the squamosal border of the parietal bone, forming the squamous suture.

The jugular tubercles are the two small, oval prominences found along the superior aspects of the lateral parts of the occipital bone. They are located:

—medial to the jugular foramina;

—lateral to the foramen magnum;

—superior to the hypoglossal canals.

Each jugular tubercle consists of a groove that lodges the glossopharyngeal, vagus and accessory nerves.

The petrous part is the medially located, dense, pyramidal portion of the temporal bone. It is continuous laterally with the squamous, mastoid and tympanic parts of the temporal bone. 

The petrous part consists of:

—a base, which is fused with the squamous, mastoid and tympanic parts;

—an apex;

—three surfaces (anterior, posterior, and inferior);

—three borders (anterior, posterior, and superior);

—the carotid canal and internal acoustic meatus;

—the structures of the internal ear, including the bony labyrinth, cochlea and semicircular canals. 

The petrous part articulates:

—anteriorly, with the greater wing of sphenoid bone, forming the sphenopetrosal synchondrosis;

—medially, with both the basilar and lateral parts of the occipital bone, forming the petrooccipital synchondrosis.

The tuberculum sellae (sellar tubercle) is the horizontal ridge found along the upper aspect of the body of sphenoid bone. The tuberculum sellae forms the anterior portion of the sella turcica and is located:

—anterior to the hypophyseal fossa;

—posterior to the chiasmatic sulcus;

—medial to both the right and left anterior clinoid processes.

In some individuals, the lateral ends of the tuberculum sellae consist of small projections, known as middle clinoid processes.

The chiasmatic sulcus (chiasmatic groove, prechiasmatic sulcus, or prechiasmatic groove) is the horizontal, shallow depression found along the upper aspect of the body of sphenoid bone. The chiasmatic sulcus:

—is located anterior to the tuberculum sellae of the sella turcica;

—is separated from the anteriorly located sphenoidal yoke by the limbus of sphenoid bone;

—is continuous laterally with both the right and left optic canals;

—lodges the optic chiasm.

The jugular fossa is a deep depression found along the inferior surface of the petrous part of temporal bone. It is located:

—posterior to the lateral end of the carotid canal;

—medial to the styloid process;

—lateral to the occipital bone.

The jugular fossa lodges the superior bulb of the internal jugular vein and both it and the jugular notch form the anterior margin of the jugular foramen.

The occipital condyles are the two oval, downwardly projecting processes found along the inferior aspects of the lateral parts of the occipital bone. They are located:

—anterior to the condylar fossae;

—medial to the jugular processes;

—lateral to the foramen magnum.

The occipital condyles provide attachment sites for the alar ligaments and consist of convex articular facets that face inferolaterally. They articulate with the superior articular facets of the atlas (first cervical vertebra), forming the atlantooccipital joints.

The nucleus of the lens is its central core. It is composed of lens cells that have, during maturation, lost their organelles and nuclei and accumulated lens crystallins arranged in a regular geometry. Thus, the lens retains all of the cells formed during life. Old cells are not discarded, they are packed into the center of the lens. Therefore, the lens is a layered structure, with fibers arranged radially like the layers of an onion. The layers represent successive generations of lens fibers formed during life. 

The nuclear region is surrounded by the cortex where the most immature lens cells are located (Taylor et al., 1996). The nuclear region can be subdivided into four zones, from superficial (adjacent to the cortex) to deep:

—embryonic (cells formed in the first six weeks after fertilization);

—fetal (cells from the seventh week of development until birth); 

—juvenile (cells formed from birth to puberty); 

—adult (cells formed from puberty).

The calcaneal tuberosity (posterior process of calcaneus) is the large, rounded, posterior end of the calcaneus. It forms the heel, contains medial and lateral processes, and provides:

—origin sites for the abductor hallucis, abductor digiti minimi, flexor digitorum brevis and quadratus plantae muscles;

—insertion sites for the gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris muscles, all via the calcaneal tendon;

—attachment sites for the plantar aponeurosis, long plantar ligament and the flexor retinaculum of foot.

The sustentaculum tali (talar shelf) is the large, shelf-like projection found along the junction between the dorsal and medial surfaces of the calcaneus. It supports the head of talus and contains the middle talar articular facet and the groove for flexor hallucis longus muscle. It provides attachment sites for the:

—plantar calcaneonavicular ligament;

—medial talocalcaneal ligament;

—tibiocalcaneal part of medial collateral ligament of ankle joint.

The neck of talus is the short, constricted section that extends from the head to the body of the bone. It contains the talar sulcus, and provides attachment sites for the:

—talonavicular ligament;

—talocalcaneal interosseous ligament;

—anterior talofibular ligament;

—anterior tibiotalar part of the medial collateral ligament of ankle joint.

The head of talus is the expanded, rounded, anterior part of the bone. It is the distal continuation of its neck and contains the facet for the plantar calcaneonavicular ligament and three articular areas that contribute to the formation of the talocalcaneonavicular joint:

—navicular articular surface of talus;

—anterior calcaneal articular facet;

—middle calcaneal articular facet.

The medial surface is the concave area on the medial aspect of the calcaneus. It is one of the six surfaces of the calcaneus, the other five being the posterior, plantar, dorsal and lateral surfaces, and the cuboid articular surface.

The medial surface of calcaneus contains the sustentaculum tali and the groove for tendon of flexor hallucis longus muscle. It provides attachment sites for the:

- plantar calcaneonavicular ligament;

- medial talocalcaneal ligament;

- tibiocalcaneal part of medial collateral ligament of ankle joint;

- flexor retinaculum of foot.

The proximal part (end) of tibia is the largely broader, superior portion of the bone. It is continuous inferiorly with the body of tibia and contains the:

—medial and lateral condyles;

—anterior and posterior intercondylar areas;

—intercondylar eminence;

—tibial tuberosity;

—tubercle of iliotibial tract.

Its superior surface is somewhat flat, known as the tibial plateau, and this articulates with the condyles of the femur, contributing to the formation of the knee joint. 

The proximal part of tibia also contains the fibular articular facet, which articulates with the head of fibula, forming the superior tibiofibular joint.

The body (shaft) of fibula is the long, tubular part of the bone. It is located between the neck and lateral malleolus of the fibula, is triangular in cross-section, and consists of:

- three surfaces (medial, lateral and posterior);

- three borders (anterior, posterior and interosseous).

The neck of femur (femoral neck) is the short, constricted, bar-like part of the bone that connects the head and body of femur. It projects superomedially from the proximal end of the body of femur, with its lateral end being the widest, and its middle part being the narrowest. The boundary that separates the neck and body of femur is defined:

—anteriorly, by the intertrochanteric line;

—posteriorly, by the intertrochanteric crest;

—superiorly, by the greater trochanter;

—inferiorly, by the lesser trochanter.

The posterior aspect of the neck of femur contains a groove, through which the tendon for the obturator externus passes. The neck of femur is the weakest part of the bone, and is therefore susceptible to injury, especially in the elderly.

The body (shaft) of femur is the long, tubular part of the bone. It is continuous superiorly with the neck of femur, and continuous inferiorly with the medial and lateral condyles of femur. It is narrowest at its middle area and expands at both its proximal and distal ends. The boundary that separates the body and neck of femur is defined:

—anteriorly, by the intertrochanteric line; 

—posteriorly, by the intertrochanteric crest;

—superiorly, by the greater trochanter;

—inferiorly, by the lesser trochanter.

The body of femur consists of:

—five surfaces; anterior, lateral, medial, posterior, and popliteal surfaces;

—seven borders; medial and lateral borders, linea aspera, spiral line, gluteal tuberosity, and the medial and lateral supracondylar lines.

The greater trochanter is the large, blunt, quadrilateral eminence on the proximal end of the femur. It projects superiorly from the superior aspect of the junction between the neck and body of femur.

The greater trochanter consists of the trochanteric fossa, provides an origin site for the vastus lateralis muscle, and provides insertion sites for the:

—gluteus minimus muscle, which inserts onto its anterior aspect;

—obturator internus and gemelli muscles, which insert onto its medial aspect;

—gluteus medius, muscle which inserts onto its lateral aspect;

—piriformis muscle, which inserts onto its superior aspect.

The linea aspera is the rough, broad ridge that travels down along the posterior aspect of the body of femur. It consists of medial and lateral lips, with an intermediate zone located between. It ends:

—superiorly, by dividing into the spiral line, gluteal tuberosity, and pectineal line of femur;

—inferiorly, by dividing into the medial and lateral supracondylar lines.

The linea aspera provides:

—origin sites for the vastus medialis, vastus lateralis, and short head of biceps femoris muscles;

—insertion sites for the adductor brevis, longus, and magnus muscles;

—attachment sites for both the medial and lateral femoral intermuscular septa.

The two muscles that collectively form the iliopsoas muscle (i.e. the psoas major and iliacus muscles) have the following insertion sites:
- the tendon of the psoas major muscle inserts onto the lesser trochanter of femur;
- the iliacus muscle inserts indirectly onto the lesser trochanter of femur by attaching to the tendon of the psoas major.

The ischial tuberosity is the large, rounded, rough elevation on the dorsal surface of body of ischium. It provides the origin sites for multiple muscles, including the:

—hamstring muscles (i.e., semitendinosus, semimembranosus and long head of biceps femoris muscles);

—ischiocondylar part (hamstring part) of adductor magnus muscle;

—quadratus femoris muscle;

—inferior gemellus muscle.

The ischial tuberosity also provides an attachment site for the sacrotuberous ligament and is the structure that supports the body when sitting.

The popliteal surface of femur is the smooth, triangular area on the posteroinferior aspect of the body of femur. It is one of the five surfaces of the body of femur, the other four being the anterior, lateral, medial, and posterior surfaces. It is an area that is bounded:

—medially, by the medial supracondylar ridge;

—laterally, by the lateral supracondylar ridge;

—inferiorly, by the intercondylar line.

The popliteal surface of femur contributes to the formation of the floor of the popliteal fossa and provides an origin site for the medial head of gastrocnemius muscle.

The cuneiform part is the flat, wedge-like, vertical plate of bone that forms the anteroinferior part of the vomer. It is continuous posterosuperiorly with both the right and left alae of vomer, contributes to the formation of the bony nasal septum and articulates:

—anterosuperiorly, with the perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone;

—anteroinferiorly, with the nasal crests of both the right and left maxillae;

—posteroinferiorly, with the nasal crests of both the right and left palatine bones.

The anterior border (anterior margin) is the long, thin, oblique margin of the cuneiform part of vomer. It is one of the four borders of the vomer, the other three being the posterior, superior and inferior borders.

The anterior border of vomer:

—consists of the vomerine groove;

—provides an attachment site for the septal nasal cartilage;

—articulates superiorly with the perpendicular plate of ethmoid bone;

—articulates inferiorly with the incisive crest of maxilla.

The zygomatic process (malar process) is the thick, rough, lateral projection found along the superolateral aspect of the maxilla. The zygomatic process:

—is continuous medially with the body of maxilla;

—articulates with the maxillary process of zygomatic bone, forming the zygomaticomaxillary suture;

—contributes to the formation of the floor of the orbit.

The lacrimal groove is the vertical, deep depression found along the nasal surface of the maxilla. It is located:

—anterior to the maxillary hiatus;

—posterior to the conchal crest of maxilla;

—anterolateral to both the lacrimal groove of lacrimal bone and the lacrimal process of inferior nasal concha;

—inferior to the lacrimal notch of maxilla.

The lacrimal groove is converted into the nasolacrimal canal by its combination with both the lacrimal groove of the lacrimal bone and the lacrimal process of the inferior nasal concha. This canal allows for the passage of the nasolacrimal duct.

The olecranon (olecranon process) is the large, superior projection on the proximal part of ulna. It curves anteriorly at its top, forming a prominent tip which is accommodated by the olecranon fossa of humerus during forearm extension.

The anterior aspect of the olecranon contains the proximal portion of the trochlear notch. The olecranon provides:

—an origin site for the ulnar head of flexor carpi ulnaris;

—insertion sites for the anconeus muscle and the tendon of the triceps brachii muscle;

—an attachment site for the ulnar collateral ligament.

The supraspinous fossa is the upper, deeply concave area on the dorsal surface of the scapula. It is located superior to the spine of the scapula and is the smaller of the two fossae found on the dorsal surface, the other being the infraspinous fossa.

The supraspinous fossa occupies almost one third of the dorsal surface of the scapula and is bounded: —medially, by the upper portion of the medial border of scapula;

—laterally, by the coracoid process;

—superiorly, by the superior angle of scapula, superior border of scapula, and superior scapular notch;

—inferiorly, by the spine of scapula.

It provides an origin site for the supraspinatus muscle.

The infraspinous fossa is the lower, slightly concave area on the dorsal surface of the scapula. It is located inferior to the spine of the scapula and is the larger of the two fossae found on the dorsal surface, the other being the supraspinous fossa.

The infraspinous fossa occupies almost two thirds of the dorsal surface of the scapula and is bounded:

—medially, by the lower portion of the medial border of scapula;

—laterally, by the lateral border of scapula;

—superiorly, by the spine of scapula;

—inferiorly, by the inferior angle of scapula.

It contains the nutrient foramen of the scapula and provides the origin site for the infraspinatus muscle.

The acromion (acromial process) is the anterior projection from the lateral end of the spine of the scapula. It is continuous medially with the spine of the scapula at the acromial angle and extends anteriorly over the glenohumeral (shoulder) joint.

The acromion consists of medial and lateral borders, which define its superior and inferior surfaces:

—the medial border contains the clavicular facet and provides an insertion site for the trapezius muscle;

—the lateral border is subcutaneous and provides an origin site for the acromial part of deltoid muscle;

—the superior surface is subcutaneous and rough;

—the inferior surface is smooth, is located superior to the glenohumeral joint and provides an attachment site for the coracoacromial ligament.

The greater tubercle (greater tuberosity) is the large eminence found on the lateral aspect of the proximal end of the humerus. It projects laterally from the junction between the anatomical neck and body of the humerus. It is located posterolateral to the lesser tubercle, with these two tubercles being separated from each other by the intertubercular sulcus. 

The greater tubercle contains an anteromedially located crest and three different facets that provide insertion sites for the following muscles: 

—supraspinatus muscle, which inserts on its superior facet;

—infraspinatus muscle, which inserts on its middle facet;

—teres minor muscle, which inserts on its inferior facet.

The coronoid process of ulna is the broad, anterior eminence on the proximal part of ulna. The superior aspect of the coronoid process contains the distal portion of the trochlear notch, its lateral aspect contains the radial notch, and its medial aspect contains the sublime tubercle.

The coronoid process provides:

—origin sites for the pronator teres and flexor digitorum profundus muscles, and the humeroulnar head of flexor digitorum superficialis muscle;

—an insertion site for the brachialis muscle;

—an attachment site for the ulnar collateral ligament.

The head of ulna is the round, expanded portion on the distal part of the bone. It contains two articular areas:

—the articular circumference of head of ulna, which articulates with the ulnar notch of the radius, forming the distal radioulnar joint;

—the articular facet of head of ulna, which attaches to the articular disc of the distal radioulnar joint.

The base of capitate bone is the quadrilateral, distal end of the bone. It contains three articular facets:

—the laterally located second metacarpal articular facet of capitate bone;

—the centrally located third metacarpal articular facet of capitate bone;

—the medially located fourth metacarpal articular facet of capitate bone.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the second thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of superior costal facets;

—a pair of inferior costal facets.

The vertebral body of the second thoracic vertebra provides origin site for inferior oblique parts of longus cervicis muscles, as well as attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the fifth thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of superior costal facets;

—a pair of inferior costal facets.

The vertebral body of the fifth thoracic vertebra provides attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The vertebral body is the small, anteriorly located cylindrical part of the fifth cervical vertebra. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. Both its superior and inferior surfaces are saddle-shaped. The pedicles extend from its posterolateral aspects and the anterior roots of the transverse processes extend from its lateral aspects. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—a pair of uncinate processes;

—vascular foramina.

The vertebral body of the fifth cervical vertebra provides an origin site for the vertical part of longus cervicis muscle, as well as attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints.

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The posterior arch is the curved, posteromedially located part of the atlas (first cervical vertebra). It is the longer of the two arches of the atlas, the other being the anterior arch. The posterior arch:

—is continuous anterolaterally with both the right and left lateral masses of atlas;

—consists of the posterior tubercle of atlas and both the right and left grooves for vertebral arteries;

—provides origin sites for both the right and left rectus capitis posterior minor muscles;

—provides attachment sites for the nuchal ligament, ligamenta flava and posterior atlantooccipital membrane.

The anterior arch is the short, curved, anteromedially located part of the atlas (first cervical vertebra). It is the shorter of the two arches of the atlas, the other being the posterior arch. The anterior arch:

—is continuous posterolaterally with both the right and left lateral masses of atlas;

—consists of the anterior tubercle of atlas and the articular facet of dens;

—provides insertion sites for the superior oblique parts of both the right and left longus cervicis muscles;

—provides attachment sites for the anterior longitudinal ligament and anterior atlantooccipital membrane;

—articulates posteriorly with the dens axis, forming the median atlantoaxial joint.

The lateral masses are the two expanded, oval, laterally located parts of the atlas (first cervical vertebra). They are both continuous:

—anteromedially, with the anterior arch of atlas;

—posteromedially, with the posterior arch of atlas;

—laterally, with a transverse process.

On its corresponding side, each lateral mass of atlas:

—consists of superior and inferior articular facets and a transverse ligament tubercle; —provides an origin site for a rectus capitis anterior muscle and an attachment site for the transverse ligament of atlas;

—articulates superiorly with an occipital condyle, forming an atlantooccipital joint;

—articulates inferiorly with a superior articular process of the axis, forming a lateral atlantoaxial joint.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the third thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of superior costal facets;

—a pair of inferior costal facets.

The vertebral body of a third thoracic vertebra provides origin site for inferior oblique parts of longus cervicis muscles, as well as attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the twelfth thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of costal facets.

The vertebral body of the twelfth thoracic vertebra provides an origin site for the psoas major and psoas minor muscles, as well as attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The xiphoid bone (xiphoid bone, xiphisternum or ensiform bone) is the thin, triangular, lower part of the sternum. It is located at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra. Its shape can vary, where it can also appear as curved or bifid, and it contains: 

—two surfaces (anterior and posterior);

—three borders (superior and two lateral).

The xiphoid process articulates with the:

—body of the sternum, forming the xiphisternal joint;

—costal cartilages of the right and left seventh ribs, forming the seventh costosternal joints.

The mastoid part is the posterolaterally located, thick portion of the temporal bone. It is continuous:

—anteromedially, with the petrous part;

—anteriorly, with both the squamous and tympanic parts. 

The mastoid part consists of:

—two surfaces (internal and external);

—two borders (parietal and occipital);

—a mastoid antrum and air cells;

—mastoid and stylomastoid foramina.

The mastoid part articulates:

—superiorly, with the mastoid angle of the parietal bone, forming the parietomastoid suture;

—posteriorly, with the mastoid border of the occipital bone, forming the occipitomastoid suture.

The styloid process is the long, pointed, inferior projection found along the inferior aspect of the temporal bone. It is located:

—anterior to the stylomastoid foramen;

—anteromedial to the mastoid process;

—medial to the mandibular fossa;

—lateral to the jugular fossa.

Its superior end is surrounded by the sheath of the styloid process, which extends inferiorly from the tympanic part of temporal bone.

The styloid process of temporal bone provides origin sites for the stylopharyngeus, styloglossus and stylohyoid muscles, as well as attachment sites for the stylomandibular and stylohyoid ligaments.

The lateral surface (orbital surface) is the superiorly located area found along the lateral aspect of the lacrimal bone. The lateral wall of lacrimal bone:

—consists of the posterior lacrimal crest and the superior portion of the lacrimal groove;

—provides an origin site for the orbicularis oculi muscle;

—contributes to the formation of the medial wall of the orbital cavity.

The posterior lacrimal crest is the vertical, anterolaterally located ridge found along the lateral surface of the lacrimal bone. The posterior lacrimal crest:

—is located posterior to the lacrimal groove of lacrimal bone;

—is continuous inferiorly with the lacrimal hamulus;

—provides an origin site for the orbicularis oculi muscle.

The sphenoidal process is the thin, posteromedial projection of the palatine bone. It extends from the posterosuperior end of the perpendicular plate of palatine bone and is located posterior to the orbital process. The sphenoidal process:

—articulates with the sphenoid bone;

—contributes to the formation of the roof of the nasal cavity.

The orbital process is the expanded, cube-like, superior projection of the palatine bone. It extends from the superior end of the perpendicular plate of palatine bone and is located anterior to the sphenoidal process. The orbital process:

—consists of an orbital surface and an air sinus;

—articulates with the maxilla and ethmoid bone;

—contributes to the formation of the floor of the orbit.

The mastoid process is the large, blunt, anteroinferior projection found along the external surface of the mastoid part of temporal bone. It is located posterior to the external acoustic meatus and posterolateral to the styloid process.

The mastoid process:

—consists of the mastoid notch;

—provides an origin site for the posterior belly of digastric muscle;

—provides insertion sites for the splenius capitis, longissimus capitis and sternocleidomastoid muscles;

—contributes to the formation of the posterior wall of the infratemporal fossa;

—is more pronounced in males than females.

The manubrium is the wide, quadrilateral, upper part of the sternum. It is located at the level of the third and fourth thoracic vertebrae and contains two surfaces (anterior and posterior) and four borders (superior, inferior and two lateral).

The manubrium of the sternum articulates with the:

—body of the sternum, forming the manubriosternal joint;

—right and left clavicles, forming the sternoclavicular joints;

—costal cartilages of the right and left first ribs, forming the first costosternal joints;

—costal cartilages of the right and left second ribs, forming the second costosternal joints.

The body (gladiolus) is the long, narrow, middle part of the sternum. It is located between the manubrium and xiphoid process of the sternum, at the level of the fifth to ninth thoracic vertebrae, and contains:

—two surfaces (anterior and posterior);

—Four borders (superior, inferior and two lateral).

The body of the sternum articulates with the:

—manubrium of the sternum, forming the manubriosternal joint;

—xiphoid process, forming the xiphisternal joint;

—costal cartilages of the right and left second to seventh ribs, forming the second to seventh costosternal joints.

The body of the sternum is longer in males than females.

The root (anatomical root) is the inferior portion of the mandibular central incisor tooth that is covered by cementum. It is separated from the crown by the cervical line, consists of one apex and is lodged in a dental alveolus of the mandible.

For descriptive purposes, the root of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. From superior to inferior, these are the:

—cervical third;

—middle third;

—apical third.

The clinical root is a term used to describe the inferior portion of a mandibular tooth that is covered by gingiva when viewed in the oral cavity.

The lingual (palatal) surface is the aspect of the maxillary central incisor tooth that faces towards the tongue and oral cavity. It is one of four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, mesial, and distal surfaces. The lingual surface consists of a cingulum, marginal ridges, and lingual fossa.

The lingual surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- distal surface at the distolingual angle;

- incisal margin at the linguoincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the lingual surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the lingual surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The cingulum is the smooth convexity found along the lingual surface of the mandibular central incisor tooth. It is located:

—within the cervical third of the lingual surface of the crown;

—superior to the cervical line;

—inferior to the lingual fossa.

The cingulum is continuous both mesially and distally with the marginal ridges.

The lingual fossa is the smooth, shallow, concave area found along the lingual surface of the mandibular central incisor tooth. It is an area that is bounded:

—inferiorly, by the cingulum;

—superiorly, by the incisal margin;

—mesially and distally, by the marginal ridges.

The mesial surface is the aspect of the mandibular central incisor tooth that faces towards the opposite mandibular central incisor tooth. It is one of four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, lingual, and distal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade. 

The mesial surface meets the:

—labial surface at the mesiolabial angle;

—lingual surface at the mesiolingual angle.

For descriptive purposes, the mesial surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

—incisal third;

—middle third;

—cervical third.

Furthermore, the mesial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to labial direction, these are the:

—lingual third;

—middle third;

—labial third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

—the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

—the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The incisal margin is the inferiorly located, cutting edge of the mandibular central incisor tooth. It meets the:

—labial surface at the labioincisal angle;

—lingual surface at the linguoincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the incisal margin of a tooth can be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

—mesial third;

—middle third;

—distal third.

The labial surface is the aspect of the maxillary central incisor tooth that faces towards the lips and vestibule. It is one of the four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the lingual, mesial, and distal surfaces.

The labial surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolabial angle;

- distal surface at the distolabial angle;

- incisal margin at the labioincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the labial surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the labial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces.

The distal surface is the aspect of the maxillary central incisor tooth that faces towards the maxillary lateral incisor tooth. It is one of the four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, lingual, and mesial surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The distal surface meets the:

- labial surface at the distolabial angle;

- lingual surface at the distolingual angle.

For descriptive purposes, the distal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the distal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to labial direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- labial third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The incisal margin is the inferiorly located, cutting edge of the mandibular canine tooth. It meets the:

- labial surface at the labioincisal angle;

- lingual surface at the linguoincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the incisal margin of a tooth can be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The mesial surface is the aspect of the maxillary canine tooth that faces towards the maxillary lateral incisor tooth. It is one of four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, lingual, and distal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The mesial surface meets the:

- labial surface at the mesiolabial angle;

- lingual surface at the mesiolingual angle.

For descriptive purposes, the mesial surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the mesial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to labial direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- labial third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The labial surface is the aspect of the maxillary canine tooth that faces towards the lips and vestibule. It is one of the four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the lingual, mesial, and distal surfaces. The labial surface consists of three labial lobes.

The labial surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolabial angle;

- distal surface at the distolabial angle;

- incisal margin at the labioincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the labial surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the labial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces.

The distal surface is the aspect of the maxillary canine tooth that faces towards the maxillary first premolar tooth. It is one of the four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, lingual, and mesial surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The distal surface meets the:

- labial surface at the distolabial angle;

- lingual surface at the distolingual angle.

For descriptive purposes, the distal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the distal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to labial direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- labial third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The lingual fossae are the two smooth, shallow, concave areas found along the lingual surface of the mandibular canine tooth.

The mesial lingual fossa is an area that is bounded:

- inferiorly, by the cingulum;

- superiorly, by the incisal margin;

- mesially, by the mesial marginal ridge;

- distally, by the lingual ridge.

The distal lingual fossa is an area that is bounded:

- inferiorly, by the cingulum;

- superiorly, by the incisal margin;

- mesially, by the lingual ridge;

- distally, by the distal marginal ridge.

The root (anatomical root) is the inferior portion of the mandibular canine tooth that is covered by cementum. It is separated from the crown by the cervical line, consists of one apex and is lodged in a dental alveolus of the mandible.

For descriptive purposes, the root of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. From superior to inferior, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- apical third.

The clinical root is a term used to describe the inferior portion of a mandibular tooth that is covered by gingiva when viewed in the oral cavity.

The cingulum is the large convexity found along the lingual surface of the maxillary canine tooth. It is located:

- within the cervical third of the lingual surface of the crown;

- inferior to the cervical line;

- superior to the lingual fossae.

The cingulum is continuous:

- both mesially and distally, with the marginal ridges;

- inferiorly, with the lingual ridge.

The lingual surface is the aspect of the mandibular canine tooth that faces towards the tongue and oral cavity. It is one of four surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other three being the labial, mesial, and distal surfaces. The lingual surface consists of a cingulum, marginal and lingual ridges, and lingual fossae.

The lingual surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- distal surface at the distolingual angle;

- incisal margin at the linguoincisal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the lingual surface of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- incisal third.

Furthermore, the lingual surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The occlusal (masticatory) surface is the aspect of the maxillary first molar tooth that faces the teeth of the mandibular dental arcade and comes into contact with them (occlusion) during mastication. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, mesial, and distal surfaces. The occlusal surface consists of:

- mesiobuccal, mesiolingual, distobuccal and distolingual cusps;

- marginal and oblique ridges;

- central, mesial triangular, distal triangular and distal fossae.

The occlusal surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the buccoocclusal angle;

- lingual surface at the linguoocclusal angle;

- mesial surface at the mesioocclusal angle;

- distal surface at the distoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the occlusal surface of the maxillary molar teeth can be divided into three portions by two horizontal lines that travel mesiodistally. In a buccal to lingual direction, these are the:

- buccal third;

- middle third;

- lingual third.

Furthermore, the occlusal surface of the maxillary molar teeth can also be divided into three portions by two horizontal lines that travel buccolingually. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The distal surface is the aspect of the maxillary first molar tooth that faces towards the maxillary second molar tooth. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, mesial, and occlusal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The distal surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the distobuccal angle;

- lingual surface at the distolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the distoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the distal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the distal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to buccal direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- buccal third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The lingual (palatal) surface is the aspect of the maxillary first molar tooth that faces towards the tongue and oral cavity. It is one of five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, mesial, distal, and occlusal surfaces.

The lingual surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- distal surface at the distolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the linguoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the lingual surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the lingual surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

The buccal surface is the aspect of the maxillary first molar tooth that faces towards the cheek and vestibule. It is one of the five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the lingual, mesial, distal, and occlusal surfaces.

The buccal surface meets the:

- mesial surface at the mesiobuccal angle;

- distal surface at the distobuccal angle;

- occlusal surface at the buccoocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the buccal surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the buccal surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a mesial to distal direction, these are the:

- mesial third;

- middle third;

- distal third.

Overall, the buccal surfaces of the posterior teeth (premolars and molars) are the equivalents to the labial surfaces of the anterior teeth (incisors and canines). Collectively, the labial and buccal surfaces are known as the vestibular surfaces, or facial surfaces.

The mesial surface is the aspect of the maxillary first molar tooth that faces towards the maxillary second premolar tooth. It is one of five surfaces of the crown of the tooth, the other four being the buccal, lingual, distal, and occlusal surfaces. It consists of a contact zone, which is the area of the tooth that touches the adjacent tooth of the same dental arcade.

The mesial surface meets the:

- buccal surface at the mesiobuccal angle;

- lingual surface at the mesiolingual angle;

- occlusal surface at the mesioocclusal angle.

For descriptive purposes, the mesial surface of a maxillary tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. In a superior to inferior direction, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- occlusal third.

Furthermore, the mesial surface of a tooth can also be divided by two vertical lines into three portions. In a lingual to buccal direction, these are the:

- lingual third;

- middle third;

- buccal third.

Overall, the mesial and distal surfaces of all teeth are collectively known as approximal surfaces, or proximal surfaces. The approximal surfaces are the surfaces of a tooth that face adjacent teeth of the same dental arcade. Each tooth has two approximal surfaces:

- the approximal surface that lies closest to the midline is known as the mesial surface;

- the approximal surface that lies furthest away from the midline is known as the distal surface.

The root (anatomical root) is the inferior portion of the mandibular first molar tooth that is covered by cementum. It is separated from the crown by the cervical line, consists of two apices (mesial and distal roots) and is lodged in the dental alveoli of the mandible.

For descriptive purposes, the root of a mandibular tooth can be divided by two horizontal lines into three portions. From superior to inferior, these are the:

- cervical third;

- middle third;

- apical third.

The clinical root is a term used to describe the inferior portion of a mandibular tooth that is covered by gingiva when viewed in the oral cavity.

The temporal process is the thin, posterolateral projection found on the posterior aspect of the zygomatic bone. The temporal process:

—provides an origin site for the masseter muscle and an attachment site for the temporal fascia;

—articulates with the zygomatic process of temporal bone, forming the temporozygomatic suture;

—forms the anterior portion of the zygomatic arch, while the zygomatic process of temporal bone forms its posterior portion.

The tympanic part (tympanic plate) is the inferiorly located, curved plate of the temporal bone. It is continuous:

—medially, with the petrous part;

—superiorly, with the squamous part;

—posteriorly, with the mastoid part. 

The tympanic part consists of the sheath of the styloid process and forms the:

—posterior, nonarticular portion of the mandibular fossa;

—anterior, inferior and posteroinferior walls of the external acoustic meatus.

The tympanic part provides attachment sites for the tympanic membrane and the cartilaginous part of the external acoustic meatus. It also accommodates a portion of the parotid gland.

The iliac fossa is the smooth, large, concave area of the inner aspect of the ilium. It is one of the three surfaces of the ilium, the other two being the sacropelvic and gluteal surfaces, and is an area that is bounded:

—anteriorly, by the anterior border of ilium;

—posteriorly, by the medial border of ilium;

—superiorly, by the iliac crest.

The iliac fossa is continuous anteroinferiorly with the pectineal surface of superior pubic ramus and provides an origin site for the iliacus muscle.

The mylohyoid grooves (mylohyoid sulci) are the two oblique depressions found along the medial surfaces of each ramus of the mandible. On its corresponding side, each mylohyoid groove:

—extends anteroinferiorly from a mandibular foramen;

—is located posterior to a mylohyoid line;

—lodges a nerve to mylohyoid muscle and mylohyoid branches of inferior alveolar vessels.

The iliac tuberosity is the rough, superiorly located area of the sacropelvic surface of ilium. It is an area that is bounded:

—anteriorly, by the medial border of ilium;

—posterosuperiorly, by the iliac crest;

—inferiorly, by the auricular surface of ilium.

The iliac tuberosity provides attachment sites for the posterior and interosseous sacroiliac ligaments.

The median part of sacrum is the centrally located part of the bone. It is formed by the fused bodies, pedicles, laminae, articular processes and spinous process of the five sacral vertebrae. It is one of three parts of the sacrum, the other two being the right and left lateral parts of sacrum. 

The median part of sacrum consists of the:

—median and intermediate sacral crests;

—promontory, superior articular processes and transverse ridges;

—sacral canal and sacral hiatus.

The sacropelvic surface of ilium is the rough, irregularly shaped area of the inner aspect of the ilium. It is one of the three surfaces of the ilium, the other two being the iliac fossa and the gluteal surface of ilium, and is an area that is bounded:

- anteriorly, by the medial border of ilium;

- posteriorly, by the posterior border of ilium;

- superiorly, by the iliac crest.

The sacropelvic surface of ilium is continuous inferiorly with the pelvic surfaces of both the body of ischium and superior pubic ramus. It consists of two sacral and pelvic portions, where:

- the sacral portion is the posterosuperiorly located portion that consists of the iliac tuberosity and the auricular surface of ilium;

- the pelvic portion is the anteroinferiorly located portion that provides an origin site for the obturator internus muscle and an attachment site for the anterior sacroiliac ligament.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the tenth thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of costal facets.

The vertebral body of the tenth thoracic vertebra provides attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The transverse ridges (lines) of the sternum are the three horizontal linear elevations that cross the anterior surface of the bone, where:

—the superior transverse ridge is located between the right to left third costal notches;

—the middle transverse ridge is located between the right to left fourth costal notches;

—the inferior transverse ridge is located between the right to left fifth costal notches.

The transverse ridges of the sternum each mark the sites of fusion between the four sternebrae that unite to form the body of the sternum during its development.

The vertebral body is the anteriorly located cylindrical part of the sixth thoracic vertebra. When viewed from above, it appears heart-shaped. Its anterior aspect is concave, while its posterior aspect, which forms the anterior wall of the vertebral foramen, is flat. It is connected to the vertebral arch via two pedicles, which extend from the upper corners of its posterior aspect. It consists of:

—superior and inferior intervertebral surfaces;

—superior and inferior annular epiphyses;

—vascular foramina;

—a pair of superior costal facets;

—a pair of inferior costal facets.

The vertebral body of the sixth thoracic vertebra provides attachment sites for the anterior and posterior longitudinal ligaments, and the radiate ligament of the head of the rib.

Overall, the sizes of the vertebral bodies gradually increase caudally along the vertebral column. Adjacent vertebral bodies articulate with each other via intervertebral discs, forming the intervertebral symphysis joints. 

In terms of function, each vertebral body provides structural support to the body weight that lies superior to it.

The temporal fossa is the large, shallow depression found along the lateral aspect of the cranium. It is formed by the frontal, parietal, temporal, sphenoid and zygomatic bones and is an area that is bounded:

- anteroinferiorly, by both the zygomatic arch and the frontal process of the zygomatic bone;

- anterosuperiorly, by the temporal line of the frontal bone;

- posterosuperiorly, by the inferior temporal line of the parietal bone;

- posteroinferiorly, by the supramastoid crest of the temporal bone.

The temporal fossa is continuous inferiorly with the infratemporal fossa, consists of the pterion, and provides an origin site for the temporalis muscle.

The temporal fossa is the large, shallow depression found along the lateral aspect of the cranium. It is formed by the frontal, parietal, temporal, sphenoid and zygomatic bones and is an area that is bounded:

- anteroinferiorly, by both the zygomatic arch and the frontal process of the zygomatic bone;

- anterosuperiorly, by the temporal line of the frontal bone;

- posterosuperiorly, by the inferior temporal line of the parietal bone;

- posteroinferiorly, by the supramastoid crest of the temporal bone.

The temporal fossa is continuous inferiorly with the infratemporal fossa, consists of the pterion, and provides an origin site for the temporalis muscle.

